Pelvic Organ Prolapse
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Vaginal bulge/pressure sensation, Visible mass at introitus, Need to splint to defecate

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-030
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of pelvic pressure, an irreducible visible mass at the introitus, and acute urinary retention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a sudden worsening of a vaginal bulge that is now protruding completely outside the body, accompanied by severe discomfort and a total inability to empty the bladder for the past 8 hours. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess for prolapse incarceration/ischemia, decompress the distended bladder via catheterization, and attempt manual reduction to prevent tissue necrosis and renal injury before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The bulge has completely fallen out, it is raw and rubbing on my underwear, and I have not been able to pass a drop of urine all day.
History of Present Illness: The patient, a 72-year-old multiparous female, reports a long-standing history of a sensation of a bulge, pressure, and heaviness in the vagina, which typically worsened towards the end of the day or after prolonged standing. She routinely needs to manually splint (apply digital pressure to the perineum) to facilitate defecation. Today, after a bout of heavy coughing, a large, visible mass protruded completely from the vaginal introitus and became irreducible. Concurrently, she developed acute difficulty completely emptying the bladder, progressing to total urinary retention over the last 8 hours. She complains of severe lower back ache radiating into the pelvis, and the exposed tissue is painful and dry.
Functional Status: Immobile; sitting uncomfortably on a rolled towel, afraid to move.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute renal injury and mucosal ulceration; daughter is highly concerned.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient is visibly uncomfortable, shifting constantly, and distressed by the bladder fullness and the prolapse.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a firm, dull, palpable mass in the suprapubic region, consistent with a significantly distended bladder. Genitourinary examination reveals a massive, stage IV pelvic organ prolapse (procidentia) completely protruding past the hymenal ring. The anterior, posterior, and apical vaginal walls are everted. The exposed mucosa is thickened, dry, and mildly hyperemic, but crucially, remains pink and viable with no signs of acute ischemia or necrosis. Gentle manual reduction is attempted but is difficult due to the severe bladder distension.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; daughter is supportive and assisting with positioning.
Medication Reconciliation: No active medications contributing to retention.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Pelvic Organ Prolapse (Stage IV Procidentia) with secondary acute urinary retention - Status: Acute mechanical complication
Differentials:

Large Uterine Fibroid/Polyp - Ruled out by direct visual inspection confirming the mass consists of everted vaginal walls and cervix, not a discrete smooth tumor originating from within the os.

Incarcerated/Ischemic Prolapse - Ruled out by the presence of pink, viable mucosa without dark discoloration, foul odor, or gangrenous changes, though the risk is present if left unreduced.

Primary Neurogenic Bladder - Unlikely; the retention is clearly a mechanical obstructive consequence of the severe anterior wall prolapse (cystocele) kinking the urethra.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute mechanical complication of a chronic stage IV pelvic organ prolapse: total urinary retention secondary to extreme cystocele and urethral kinking. Objective clinical examination confirmed a massively distended bladder and a fully everted, irreducible prolapse. The absolute inability to void for 8 hours created an imminent risk of acute renal injury and extreme discomfort. Urgent after-hours intervention was mandatory to immediately catheterize and decompress the bladder. Once the bladder was emptied, the mechanical tension was released, allowing for the safe, manual reduction of the prolapse to prevent mucosal ulceration. This definitive management could not wait until the next available in-hours appointment without risking severe complications.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed a gentle, sterile in-and-out catheterization yielding 800mL of clear urine, instantly relieving the suprapubic pain. Following bladder decompression, successfully performed manual reduction of the prolapse using copious water-based lubricant, returning the organs to the pelvic cavity. Instructed the patient to remain strictly supine or in a reclined position for the remainder of the evening to prevent immediate re-prolapse. Initiated an urgent referral to a urogynecologist for definitive management (pessary fitting or surgical repair).
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent the prolapse from immediately falling out again tonight, remain completely flat in bed and avoid any straining, coughing, or heavy lifting; feeling the relief of the organs staying in place will reinforce strict adherence to bed rest. To protect the delicate vaginal skin, apply a generous amount of plain Vaseline to the area before bed; experiencing the reduction in chafing will encourage proactive tissue care.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow morning to arrange an expedited urogynecology specialist appointment.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if you become unable to pass urine again for more than 6 hours, if the prolapse falls out and becomes dark purple, black, or extremely painful, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The manual reduction is a temporary mechanical fix; gravity and straining will likely cause the prolapse to recur until definitive specialist treatment is provided.
Clinical Course: The extreme bladder discomfort has been resolved. You must rest flat to keep the organs internal until you can see a specialist for a permanent solution like a pessary or surgery.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you cannot empty your bladder again, or if the tissue falls out and turns a dark, bruised color.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The supporting muscles in your pelvis have weakened completely, allowing your bladder, bowel, and uterus to fall entirely outside your body. The weight of this mass severely kinked your urethra, blocking your urine. By draining your bladder with a tube, we removed the fluid weight, which allowed me to gently push everything back inside. You must see a specialist urgently for a permanent fix.
Actionable Steps: Lie completely flat in bed for the rest of the night. Do not strain when using the toilet. Contact your GP first thing tomorrow to fast-track your specialist appointment.

